## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of bit-level data packing and word-level efficiency.

## Key Concepts
- Bit spreading (dilating)
- Inline masks and shifts for 32-bit results
- Morton codes for spatial indexing

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t part1by1(uint16_t n) {
  uint32_t x = n;
  x &= 0x0000FFFF;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t morton2D(uint16_t x, uint16_t y) {
  return (part1by1(y) << 1) | part1by1(x);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 3D Morton codes (x,y,z) and 64-bit result? 
- How would you adapt for non-power-of-two bit widths or streaming data?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests low-level arithmetic with 128-bit values, ensuring correct carry propagation and branchless coding. It mirrors real-world bit-twiddling tasks in crypto, compression, or hash pipelines where performance and correctness matter.

## Key Concepts

- Carry propagation across 64-bit boundaries
- 128-bit arithmetic using hi/lo
- Branchless implementation using comparisons as carry indicators

## Code Example

```c
uint128 add128(uint128 a, uint128 b, unsigned *carryOut) {
  uint64_t lo = a.lo + b.lo;
  unsigned c0 = lo < a.lo;
  uint64_t hi = a.hi + b.hi + c0;
  unsigned c1 = hi < a.hi;
  *carryOut = c1;
  return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
}
```

