## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about cross-boundary bit interactions and implement constant-time checks using only bitwise ops. 

## Key Concepts
- Bitwise shifts and masks across multiword values
- Detecting adjacent bits within each 64-bit half and across the 64-bit boundary
- Branchless boolean results for interview clarity

## Code Example
```javascript
int hasAdjacentOnes128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t a = x.lo & (x.lo << 1);
  uint64_t b = x.hi & (x.hi << 1);
  int cross = ((x.lo >> 63) & 1) & (x.hi & 1);
  return (a | b | cross) != 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to detect a run of exactly k adjacent ones? 
- How would you implement a similar check for 256-bit values represented as two uint128s?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft compact, branchless bit-twiddling across a 128-bit type.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit split into hi/lo; XOR fold reduces width.
- Branchless reduction using successive shifts and XORs.
- Final parity is the low bit after folding.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Implementation in C-like syntax for readability
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
unsigned int parity128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t t = x.hi ^ x.lo;
  t ^= t >> 32;
  t ^= t >> 16;
  t ^= t >> 8;
  t ^= t >> 4;
  t ^= t >> 2;
  t ^= t >> 1;
  return (unsigned int)(t & 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you compute the full popcount for 128-bit without branches?
- How does this approach fare on different compilers and architectures?

## Why This Is Asked
Advanced bit manipulation questions assess knowledge of branchless arithmetic and de Bruijn indexing techniques.

## Key Concepts
- Bit manipulation: isolating the least significant bit using two's complement arithmetic
- De Bruijn sequence mapping for constant-time position lookup
- Branchless, constant-time operations
- Proper edge-case handling for n == 0

## Code Example
```c
int ctz64(uint64_t n) {
  // Implementation uses de Bruijn indexing
  uint64_t t = n & -n;
  // table[index] yields trailing-zero count
  return table[(t * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a01ULL) >> 58];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Transposition in bit-sliced form is a classic challenge that tests mastery of layout tricks and how data moves across a 128-bit container without branches.

## Key Concepts
- Bit-slice layout and Morton-order ideas
- Multi-stage bit redistribution with masks
- Branchless, vector-style bit manipulation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation outline for guidance
uint128 transpose8x16(uint128 x) {
  // stage 1: swap adjacent bits
  x = (x & 0x5555...5555) | ((x >> 1) & 0x5555...5555);
  // stage 2: swap pairs
  x = (x & 0x3333...3333) | ((x >> 2) & 0x3333...3333);
  // stage 3: swap nibbles
  x = (x & 0x0F0F...0F0F) | ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F...0F0F);
  // stage 4: move bytes
  x = (x & 0x00FF00FF) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF);
  return x;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of non-trivial bit interleaving without loops, a common low-level optimization in graphics/encoding.

## Key Concepts
- Bit spreading (Morton order)
- Mask-and-shift interleaving
- 128-bit composition from two 64-bit halves

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudocode outline (no branches)
uint128 interleaveBits128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  uint64_t lo = x.lo, hi = x.hi;
  uint64_t sLo = spreadBy2(lo); // using masks 0x5555.., 0x3333.., etc.
  uint64_t sHi = spreadBy2(hi);
  r.lo = sLo;
  r.hi = sHi << 1;
  return r;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your ability to perform a branchless, cross-boundary bitwise scan on a 128-bit value—a common primitive in cryptography and systems programming. It verifies you can correctly propagate carries and parity across the 64-bit lane boundary without branching.

## Key Concepts

- **Parallel prefix (scan) technique**: Each shift-XOR step doubles the bit-range being folded, achieving O(log n) instead of O(n) operations.
- **128-bit right shift implementation**: Requires explicit carry propagation between the hi and lo 64-bit words.
- **Branchless design**: No conditionals ensures constant-time execution, critical for crypto applications.

## Code Example

```javascript
function prefixXor128(x_lo, x_hi) {
  let lo = x_lo, hi = x_hi;
  // 128-bit shift helper: (lo, hi) >> k
  const shift128 = (lo, hi, k) => {
    if (k < 64) return { lo: (lo >>> k) | ((hi & ((1n << k) - 1n)) << (64 - k)), hi: hi >>> k };
    return { lo: hi >>> (k - 64), hi: 0 };
  };
  // Parallel prefix: x ^= x >> {1,2,4,8,16,32,64}
  for (const k of [1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64]) {
    const s = shift128(lo, hi, k);
    lo ^= s.lo; hi ^= s.hi;
  }
  return { lo, hi };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for a prefix OR or prefix AND?
- What changes are needed to handle SIMD/vector implementations?
- How does this compare to a naive loop-based approach in terms of cycle count?
